SET search_path = regs, public;

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- Building Act
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_ACT_1975@2026-02-01', 's 21(5)', 'SECTION',
$t$relevant provisions, for building work, means— (a) if alternative provisions under section 33, or provisions as varied under section 44 (varied provisions), apply to all or part of the building work— (i) the alternative provisions or varied provisions; and (ii) any relevant deemed-to-satisfy provision under the BCA or relevant acceptable solution under the QDC for the work, other than the QDC residential design and siting provisions; and (iii) any other building assessment provision applying to the work; or (b) if no alternative provisions under section 33, or varied provisions, apply to all or part of the building work— (i) any relevant deemed-to-satisfy provision under the BCA or relevant acceptable solution under the QDC for the work; and (ii) any other building assessment provision ...$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 38');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_ACT_1975@2026-02-01', 's 30', 'SECTION',
$t$The following laws and other documents are the building assessment provisions— (a) chapter 3 and this chapter; (b) the fire safety standard; (c) the fire safety standard (RCB); (d) any provisions of a regulation made under this Act relating to building assessment work or accepted building work; (e) any relevant local law, local planning instrument or resolution made under section 32 or any relevant provision under section 33; (f) the BCA; (g) subject to section 33, the QDC.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 43');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_ACT_1975@2026-02-01', 's 31(3)-(6)', 'SECTION',
$t$(3) The effect of a building assessment provision mentioned in section 30(a) to (d), (f) or (g) can not be changed under a local law, local planning instrument or local government resolution. (4) A local law, local planning instrument or local government resolution must not include provisions about building work, to the extent a building assessment provision mentioned in subsection (3) applies to the building work. (5) To the extent a local law, local planning instrument or local government resolution does not comply with subsection (4), the local law, local planning instrument or local government resolution is of no effect. (6) Subsections (3) to (5) are subject to sections 32 and 33.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 44');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_ACT_1975@2026-02-01', 's 32', 'SECTION',
$t$A local government may make or amend— (a) a local planning instrument that designates, for the BCA or QDC, matters prescribed under a regulation; or Example of a matter that may be prescribed— designated bush fire prone areas for the BCA (b) a provision of a local law or planning scheme or a resolution about an aspect of, or matter related or incidental to, building work prescribed under a regulation; or Examples of aspects that may be prescribed— swimming pool fencing or land liable to flooding (c) alternative provisions under section 33.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 44-45');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_ACT_1975@2026-02-01', 's 33', 'SECTION',
$t$(1) This section applies for work (relevant work) that— (a) is building assessment work or accepted building work; and (b) is for a relevant building or a class 10 building or structure located on the same allotment as a single detached class 1 building. (2) A planning scheme or PDA instrument may include provisions (alternative provisions) that, for relevant work, are alternative or different to the QDC residential design and siting provisions. (3) However, a planning scheme or PDA instrument may include alternative provisions only if the provisions are a qualitative statement or quantifiable standard. (4) If there are alternative provisions for relevant work, the QDC residential design and siting provisions only apply to the extent the alternative provisions do not apply to the work. (5) Alternative provisions can not be made other than under a planning scheme or PDA instrument.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 45');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_ACT_1975@2026-02-01', 's 35', 'SECTION',
$t$If the BCA is inconsistent with a part of the QDC, the part prevails to the extent of the inconsistency.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 46');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_ACT_1975@2026-02-01', 's 36(2)', 'SECTION',
$t$Building assessment work, other than building assessment work carried out under section 46, must comply with the building assessment provisions in force when the application is approved.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 46');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_ACT_1975@2026-02-01', 's 231B', 'SECTION',
$t$(1) A regulated pool— (a) is a swimming pool situated on regulated land; and (b) includes the barriers for the pool. (2) The barriers for the pool include any of the following— (a) the fencing for the pool; (b) the walls of a building enclosing the pool; (c) another form of barrier mentioned or provided for in the pool safety standard. ... (4) Also, a regulated pool does not include a public pool.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_ACT_1975@2026-02-01', 's 231D(1)', 'SECTION',
$t$The pool safety standard is— (a) QDC, part MP3.4; and (b) any other standard prescribed under a regulation for ensuring the safety of persons using a regulated pool.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_ACT_1975@2026-02-01', 's 232(1)', 'SECTION',
$t$The owner of a regulated pool must ensure— (a) the pool complies with the pool safety standard for the pool; and (b) all barriers for the pool are kept in good condition. Maximum penalty—165 penalty units.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_ACT_1975@2026-02-01', 's 234(2)', 'SECTION',
$t$The person must, unless the person has a reasonable excuse, ensure that, before the pool is filled with water to a depth of 300mm or more, a building certifier has provided a certificate in the approved form stating the pool is a complying pool.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_ACT_1975@2026-02-01', 'sch 2 regulated land', 'DEFINITION',
$t$regulated land— 1 Regulated land is land on which any of the following is constructed or is to be constructed— (a) a class 1, 2, 3 or 4 building; (b) a moveable dwelling park; (c) a residential park. 2 The term includes land adjacent to the land and— (a) in the same ownership as the land; or (b) used in association with the land.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM');

-- ----------------------------------------------------------- Building Regulation
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_REG_2021@2026-08-14', 's 4', 'SECTION',
$t$(1) For section 21(2) of the Act— (a) the building work (accepted development (self assessable)) mentioned in schedule 1 is prescribed ...; and (b) the building work mentioned in schedule 2 is prescribed. (2) The building work mentioned in schedule 1 must comply with the relevant provisions within the meaning of section 21(5) of the Act.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 9');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_REG_2021@2026-08-14', 's 80', 'SECTION',
$t$For schedule 2 of the Act, definition QDC residential design and siting provisions, parts 1.1 and 1.2 of the QDC, mentioned in schedule 7, are prescribed.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 57');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_REG_2021@2026-08-14', 's 80A', 'SECTION',
$t$(1) For section 33(6) of the Act, definition relevant building, paragraph (b), the following buildings are prescribed— (a) 2 class 1 buildings located on the same allotment, but only if 1 of the buildings is a secondary dwelling; (b) a class 2 building but only if— (i) the building consists of a maximum of 2 dwellings; and (ii) 1 of the dwellings is a secondary dwelling.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 57');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_REG_2021@2026-08-14', 's 106', 'SECTION',
$t$(1) This section applies to a local government's planning scheme if the scheme— (a) was in effect for a local government area immediately before the commencement; or (b) is made after the commencement but before 1 September 2029. (2) Former sections 6 and 80 and former schedule 7 continue in force in relation to the local government's planning scheme until the earlier of the following days— (a) if the scheme is amended to state how the Queensland Housing Code applies in relation to the scheme—the effective day for the amendment; Note— If paragraph (a) applies to a local government's planning scheme, the department will publish the effective day on the department's website. (b) 1 September 2029.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 70', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL,
 'Former s6 text not held (pre-2026 reprint needed). Former s80/sch 7 prescribed MP 1.1 and MP 1.2 (2010) as the QDC residential design and siting provisions.');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_REG_2021@2026-08-14', 'sch 7', 'SCHEDULE_ITEM',
$t$1.1 Design and siting standard for housing on lots under 450m2 — published 3 August 2026 — took effect 1 September 2026; 1.2 Design and siting standard for housing on lots 450m2 and over — published 3 August 2026 — took effect 1 September 2026; 1.4 Building over or near relevant infrastructure — published 13 November 2014 — 15 December 2014; 3.4 Swimming pool barriers — published 18 July 2012 — 27 July 2012; 3.5 Construction of buildings in flood hazard areas — published 12 December 2013 — 20 December 2013.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 95-96', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, 'Table flattened to text; only the rows relevant to this dataset are reproduced.');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_REG_2021@2026-08-14', 'sch 1 s 1', 'SCHEDULE_ITEM',
$t$(1) Building work for a fence, if the fence is— (a) no higher than 2m above the fence's natural ground surface; and (b) not for a regulated pool.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 72');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_REG_2021@2026-08-14', 'sch 1 s 13', 'SCHEDULE_ITEM',
$t$(1) Building work for a class 10 building or structure that meets the requirements of subsection (2), other than building work— (a) within Wind Region C (tropical cyclone area) mentioned in AS 1170.2-2011 'Structural design actions Wind actions'; or (b) for a rainwater tank for a new building; or (c) for a deck that is roofed or higher than 1m above the deck's natural ground surface; or (d) for a regulated pool or its fence; or (e) for a solar hot water system or photovoltaic solar panel. (2) For subsection (1), the requirements are— (a) the plan area of the class 10 building or structure is no more than 10m2; and (b) the class 10 building or structure has, above its natural ground surface— (i) a height of no more than 2.4m; and (ii) if the class 10 building or structure is not a rainwater tank—a mean height of no more than 2.1m, worked out by dividing its total elevational area facing a boundary by its horizontal length facing the boundary; and (c) any side of the class 10 building or structure is no longer than 5m.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 79');
SELECT define_provision('QLD_BUILDING_REG_2021@2026-08-14', 'sch 2 s 1', 'SCHEDULE_ITEM',
$t$Building work for a class 10b structure or special structure if— (a) the structure is not— (i) a fence; or (ii) a retaining wall; or (iii) a free standing wall; or (iv) a swimming pool; and (b) the structure is no higher than 3m above its natural ground surface.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 81');

-- ----------------------------------------------------------- Planning Regulation
SELECT define_provision('QLD_PLANNING_REG_2017@2026-09-11', 'sch 9 pt 3 div 2 table 3', 'TABLE',
$t$Development application for building work that is assessable development under section 1, if— (a) the Queensland Development Code, part 1.1, 1.2 or 1.3 applies to the building work and, under the part, the proposed building or structure does not include an acceptable solution for a relevant performance criteria under the part; or (b) under the Building Act, section 33, an alternative provision applies for the building work and, under the provision, the proposed building or structure is not of the quantifiable standard for a relevant qualitative statement under the provision; ... Referral agency: The local government. Matters referral agency's assessment must be against: For building work stated in item 1, column 2, paragraph (a)—whether the proposed building or structure complies with the performance criteria stated in the paragraph; For ... paragraph (b)—whether the proposed building or structure complies with the qualitative statement stated in the paragraph$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM');

-- ===================================================================== QHC 2026
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 's 3', 'APPLICATION',
$t$This Queensland Housing Code was published on 03 August 2026 and commences on 01 September 2026. It replaces QDC 1.1 and QDC 1.2 published on 11 March 2010.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 2', 3);
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 's 4', 'APPLICATION',
$t$1. The QHC applies to the design and siting of new building work for: (a) a single Class 1 building; and (b) a dwelling house involving: (i) two Class 1 buildings where one of the buildings is a secondary dwelling; and (ii) a Class 2 building with a maximum of two dwellings where one of the dwellings is a secondary dwelling; and (c) associated Class 10a buildings; and (d) associated structures. 2. The QHC applies to all zones where a dwelling is a permitted use. ... 4. The QHC does not apply to: (a) structures less than 1 metre above ground level except for swimming pools, which are covered by the QHC; or (b) indoor swimming pools; or (c) building work within a tourist park; or (d) associated equipment for an electric line; or (e) development in a priority development area. 5. Chapter 1 applies to lots under 450 metres2. 6. Chapter 2 applies to lots 450 metres2 and over. 7. If the proposed building work is located entirely or partially within a relevant overlay, the requirements of the QHC will be varied by the provisions of the relevant overlay only to the extent that such variations are stated.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 2-3', 3);
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 's 7(3)', 'APPLICATION',
$t$For the purpose of this part, the following are deemed to be QDC acceptable solutions: (a) a plan of development approved by a local government that specifies matters regulated under this Part.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 4');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 's 9', 'APPLICATION',
$t$1. An exemption statement is mandatory and is provided to specify where or when a requirement or provision does not need to be complied with. 2. A supplementary provision is mandatory and provides additional direction on how to apply Tables 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, and A1. 3. Notes are not mandatory and included to provide guidance only.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 4');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'def QHC setback', 'DEFINITION',
$t$QHC setback or setback means: (a) for an external wall of a building, the shortest distance, measured horizontally, between the façade of an external wall of the building to the vertical projection of the boundary of the lot where the building is; or (b) for a garage door, the shortest distance, measured horizontally, between the garage door to the vertical projection of the boundary of the lot that provides access to vehicles; or (c) for a part of a building that is a window, deck, pergola, privacy screen, external fixture, covered outdoor room, balcony or weather control element, the shortest distance, measured horizontally, between the part of the building to the vertical projection of the boundary of the lot where the part of the building is; or (d) for a structure, other than a swimming pool, the shortest distance, measured horizontally, between the outermost projection of a structure to the vertical projection of the boundary of the lot where the structure is; or (e) for a swimming pool, the shortest distance measured horizontally from the water's edge to the vertical projection of a boundary of the lot; or (f) for a corner truncation: ...$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 13-14');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'def QHC site cover', 'DEFINITION',
$t$QHC site cover or site cover means (a) the portion of the site, expressed as a percentage, that will be covered by a building or structure and includes— (i) for enclosed spaces – the area including the external wall; or (ii) for unenclosed spaces with a roof – the area measured along a line 600mm in from the outermost projection; but (b) does not include the area of: (i) an unroofed structure; or (ii) a swimming pool; or (iii) a retaining wall; or (iv) a fence.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 14');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'def primary frontage boundary', 'DEFINITION',
$t$Primary frontage boundary of a lot is – in the following order of priority: (a) the boundary nominated by the local government; or (b) the frontage boundary nominated on an approved plan of development; or (c) the frontage boundary addressed by the adjoining premises or most commonly addressed by the nearest adjacent buildings as the frontage boundary of the lot; or (d) where a lot is not vacant, the frontage boundary to which the existing building has its assigned street address; or (e) if the lot is on a road corner, the shortest frontage boundary.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 12');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'def lot width', 'DEFINITION',
$t$Lot width means: a) the shortest distance between two side boundaries of a lot, measured at the primary frontage boundary of the lot; or b) for an irregularly shaped lot, lot width may be determined as either: i) the measurement between two side boundaries of a lot, measured at the building line; or ii) the average lot width.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 10');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'def covered outdoor room', 'DEFINITION',
$t$Covered outdoor room means an outdoor area adjoining a building, generally used to provide weather protection for an entry or for dining / recreation, that is covered with a roof, including: (a) an alfresco (b) a balcony (c) a deck (d) a pergola (e) a porch (f) a porte cochere (g) a verandah.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 7-8');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'def structure height', 'DEFINITION',
$t$Structure height means (a) for a free-standing structure - the vertical distance (in metres) from ground level to the highest point of the structure; or (b) for a structure attached to a building - the vertical distance (in metres) from the building height to the highest point of the structure.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 17');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'def mean height', 'DEFINITION',
$t$Mean height of a building means the vertical height worked out by dividing – (a) the total elevational area of the building facing the primary frontage boundary; by (b) the horizontal length of the building facing a frontage boundary.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 11');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'def carport', 'DEFINITION',
$t$Carport means an unenclosed Class 10a building providing one or more covered vehicle parking spaces and may include a garage door.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 6');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'def relevant overlay', 'DEFINITION',
$t$relevant overlay means— (a) an overlay, or part of an overlay, that is about— (i) bush fire hazards, coastal hazards, flood hazards or landslide hazards; or (ii) safety hazards arising from historic mining activities ...; or (b) an overlay, or part of an overlay, that includes an overlay code and is about— (i) development of a local heritage place; or (ii) development in a place with traditional building character; or (iii) the protection of areas of natural, environmental or ecological significance ...; or (iv) development within an area identified on a map titled 'ANEF' ...$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 15');

-- Chapter 1 (lots < 450 m2)
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch1 A1.1', 'ACCEPTABLE_SOLUTION',
$t$1. Class 1 buildings and Class 2 buildings have a maximum height of 8.5m. 2. Class 10a buildings have a maximum height of 4.5m. 3. Where the slope of a lot exceeds 15%, the heights in A1.1(1)-(2) are increased by 1.5m. 4. Structures have a maximum height of: a. for a fence, privacy fence, privacy screen, retaining wall or free-standing wall or the like – 2m; or b. for a prescribed device that is attached to a building – 3.5m above the building; or c. for a prescribed device that is detached from a building – 10m. 5. A reference to a fence, privacy fence, privacy screen retaining wall or free-standing wall includes a structure that is a combination of these elements and must not exceed a maximum height of 2m. 6. A1.1(4) does not apply to swimming pools. Note ... 1. Adopting local governments may vary the default acceptable solutions for building and structure height by including alternative provisions in the relevant local government planning scheme under section 33 of the Building Act.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 21', 22, 'Chapter 1');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch1 A2.1 Table 1', 'TABLE',
$t$Table 1 — Minimum frontage boundary setbacks for buildings [columns: Primary frontage boundary | Secondary frontage boundaries −Road | Secondary frontage boundaries −Laneway, Pedestrian lane or Parkway | Secondary frontage boundaries −Other] External wall (excluding garages and carports): 3m | 1.5m | 1m | 2m. Building work including: a garage; or a carport. Building work including: a garage door: 5m | 5m | 1m | 2m. Building work including a detached: laundry; or shed: 5.4m | 5.4m | 1m | 2m. Building work including: a carport that is 4.5m or less in height with a mean height of no more than 3.5m; or a garage door for a carport 4.5m or less in height with a mean height of no more than 3.5m; or a gatehouse that is 3m or less in height and has a roof area of no more than 4m2: BTB permitted | BTB permitted | BTB permitted | 1m. Supplementary Provisions In Table 1: 1. Where a laneway or a parkway is the primary frontage boundary of a lot, then the setback for a Class 1 building or a Class 2 building in Table 1 is 2m. 2. Where the setback of an adjoining premises is less than the primary frontage boundary setback specified in Table 1, the primary frontage boundary setback for a Class 1 building or a Class 2 building is reduced by 1m. 3. Any Class 10a building work not specifically listed in Table 1 must comply with the setbacks for a Class 1 building in Table 1. 4. Where a garage door is built to boundary, the design and operation of the door must ensure that it does not project or encroach beyond the lot boundary at any point during its operation.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 22-23', 23, 'Chapter 1', NULL, NULL,
 'Row grouping of "garage; carport" and "garage door" with the 5m row read from layout: both bullets sit against the single 5m/5m/1m/2m row.');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch1 A2.1 Table 2', 'TABLE',
$t$Table 2— Minimum frontage boundary setbacks – structures [Primary | Secondary−Road | Secondary−Laneway, Pedestrian lane or Parkway | Secondary−Other] If 1.2m or less in height and involving building work that is: a swimming pool: 1.5m | 1.5m | 1.5m | 1.5m. If 2m or less in height ... a utility meter; or a utility structure, if screened from public view: BTB permitted (all). If 2.4m or less in height ... an external fixture: 1m | 1m | 1m | 1m. If over 2.4m in height ... an external fixture: 2m | 1m | 1m | 1m. If 3.0m or less in height ... an entry structure: BTB permitted (all). If 4.5m or less in height ... a weather control element for a carport: BTB permitted | BTB permitted | 0.5m | 1m. If 4.5m or less in height ... a covered outdoor room; or a balcony; or stairs, ramps or landings: 1.5m | 0.5m | 0.5m | 1.5m. If 8.5m or less in height ... a weather control element; or a privacy screen: 1.5m | 0.5m | 0.5m | 1.5m. Supplementary Provisions In Table 2: 1. Where structures exceed the height requirements stated in Table 2, or are not specifically listed in Table 2, the setback in Table 1 for Class 1 buildings applies. 2. If a structure is attached to a building, and the setback for the building specified in Table 1 is less than the setback for the structure specified in Table 2, the setback for the building in Table 1 applies to the structure. 3. Where a solid fence extends at least 1m above the level of the swimming pool deck (between the water and the frontage), the minimum setback for a swimming pool is BTB.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 23-24', 24, 'Chapter 1');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch1 A3.1 Table 3', 'TABLE',
$t$Table 3 — Minimum common boundary setbacks – external walls [Side boundary | Rear boundary] Class 1 buildings and Class 2 buildings: The ground floor, or that part of an external wall that is 4.5m or less in height: 1m | 3m. The second storey, or that part an external wall that is over 4.5m and 8.5m or less in height: 1m | 2.5m. The third storey, or that part an external wall that is over 8.5m in height: Where the lot width is 12.5m or less: 2m | 2.5m. ... Where the lot width is more than 12.5m: 2.5m | 2.5m. Secondary dwelling (single storey detached): That part of a detached secondary dwelling that is 4.5m or less in height: 1m | 1.5m. Class 10a buildings: If 3.5m or less in height and involving building work that is: a gatehouse that has a roof area of no more than 4m2: BTB permitted | BTB permitted. If 4.5m or less in height and involving building work that is: a garage, carport, laundry, or shed up to 9m total combined length along each common boundary: BTB permitted | BTB permitted. Supplementary Provisions In Table 3: ... 3. Where Class 10a buildings exceed the height requirements stated in Table 3, or are not specifically listed in Table 3, the setbacks for Class 1 buildings in Table 3 apply. 4. Setbacks under Table 3 may be determined using either: a. the number of storeys; or b. the height of the external wall at the relevant point. 5. For Table 3, a garage, carport, laundry, or shed may extend along two intersecting common boundaries, provided the total length along each common boundary does not exceed 9m.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 25-26', 26, 'Chapter 1');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch1 A3.1 Table 4', 'TABLE',
$t$Table 4 — Minimum common boundary setbacks – structures [Side boundary | Rear boundary] If 2m or less in height ... a utility meter; or a utility structure, if screened from public view: BTB permitted | BTB permitted. If 2.4m or less in height ... an external fixture: 0.5m | 0.5m. If 3.0m or less ... an entry structure: BTB permitted | BTB permitted. If 4.5m or less ... a weather control element for a carport: BTB permitted | BTB permitted. If 4.5m or less ... a covered outdoor room; or a balcony: 0.5m | 1.5m. If 4.5m or less ... a swimming pool: 1.5m | 1.5m. If 8.5m or less ... a weather control element; or a privacy screen: 0.5m | 1.5m. Supplementary Provisions In Table 4: 1. Where a weather control element is attached to a part of a building that is BTB, the minimum setback is BTB. 2. Where structures exceed the height requirements stated in Table 4, or are not specifically listed in Table 4, the setbacks in Table 3 for Class 1 buildings and Class 2 buildings apply. 3. If a structure is attached to a building, and the setback for the building specified in Table 3 is less than the setback for the structure specified in Table 4, the setback for the building in Table 3 applies to the structure. 4. Where a solid fence extends at least 1m above the level of the swimming pool (between the water and the adjoining premises), the minimum setback for a swimming pool is BTB.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 28', 29, 'Chapter 1');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch1 A4.1', 'ACCEPTABLE_SOLUTION',
$t$1. The maximum site cover for all buildings and structures does not exceed: a. on lots 250m² or less – 65%; and b. on lots greater than 250m² – 60%. 2. When calculating site cover for a battle axe lot, the area of an access handle that forms part of the lot must be excluded from the calculation. Note 1. Adopting local governments may increase the default acceptable solutions for site cover and internal floor area by including alternative provisions in the relevant local government planning scheme under section 33 of the Building Act.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 29', 30, 'Chapter 1');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch1 A5', 'ACCEPTABLE_SOLUTION',
$t$3. Where a deck, pergola, verandah or balcony is more than 1m above ground level and is setback 2m or less from a common boundary, building work includes a privacy screen at the perimeter of the deck, pergola, verandah or balcony extending not less than 1.5m above the floor level.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 30', 31, 'Chapter 1');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch1 A6.1', 'ACCEPTABLE_SOLUTION',
$t$1. Walls within 750mm of a common boundary must be maintenance free.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 31', 32, 'Chapter 1');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch1 A10', 'ACCEPTABLE_SOLUTION',
$t$1. A fence, privacy fence, privacy screen, retaining wall or free-standing wall with a maximum height of 2m can be built to boundary. 2. A reference to a fence, privacy fence, privacy screen retaining wall or free-standing wall includes a structure that is a combination of these elements and must not exceed a maximum height of 2m. Note ... 2. Further requirements for swimming pool barriers are contained in the QDC MP 3.4.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 36', 37, 'Chapter 1');

-- Chapter 2 (lots >= 450 m2)
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch2 A1.1', 'ACCEPTABLE_SOLUTION',
$t$1. Class 1 buildings and Class 2 buildings have a maximum height of 8.5m. 2. Class 10a buildings have a maximum height of 4.5m. 3. Where the slope of a lot exceeds 15%, the heights in A1.1(1)-(2) are increased by 1.5m. 4. Structures have a maximum height of–: a. for a fence, privacy fence, privacy screen, retaining wall or free-standing wall or the like – 2m; ... 6. A1.1(4) does not apply to swimming pools.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 41', 42, 'Chapter 2');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch2 A2.1 Table 1', 'TABLE',
$t$Table 1 — Minimum frontage boundary setbacks for buildings [Primary frontage boundary | Secondary frontage boundaries −Road | Secondary −Laneway, Pedestrian lane or Parkway | Secondary −Other] External wall (excluding garages and carports): 5m | Apply Table A1 | 1m | 3m. Building work including: a garage; or a carport. Building work including: a garage door: 5.6m | 5.6m | 1m | 3m. Building work including a detached: laundry; or shed: 6m | 6m | 1m | 3m. Building work including: a carport that is 4.5m or less in height with a mean height of no more than 3.5m; or a garage door for a carport ... 4.5m or less in height with a mean height of no more than 3.5m; or a gatehouse that is 3m or less in height and has a roof area of no more than 4m2: BTB permitted | BTB permitted | BTB permitted | 1m. Supplementary Provisions In Table 1: 1. Where a laneway or a parkway is the primary frontage boundary of a lot, then the setback for a Class 1 building or a Class 2 building in Table 1 is 2m. 2. Where the setback of an adjoining premises is less than the primary frontage boundary setback specified in Table 1, the primary frontage boundary setback for a Class 1 building or a Class 2 building is reduced by 1m. 3. Any Class 10a building work not specifically listed in Table 1 must comply with the setbacks for a Class 1 building in Table 1. 4. Where a garage door is built to boundary ...$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 42-44', 43, 'Chapter 2');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch2 Table A1', 'FIGURE',
$t$Table A1 [graph: Lot width (10-24) against Minimum Secondary frontage boundaries – Road (1.5-6.0); lines "BUILDINGS ≤ 4.5M HIGH" and "BUILDINGS > 4.5M HIGH"] Supplementary Provisions For Table A1: 1. The Minimum QHC setback can be calculated using the following formulas: For buildings ≤ 4.5m high: QHC setback = (lot width – 5.34) / 3.11. For buildings > 4.5m high: QHC setback = (lot width – 4) / 3.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_FROM_FIGURE', 'Page 45', 46, 'Chapter 2', NULL, NULL,
 'Formulas are rendered as images; transcribed from a 150 dpi page render. The graph spans setbacks 1.5-6.0 m and lot widths 10-24 m; the text does not say whether results outside that range are clamped.');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch2 A2.1 Table 2', 'TABLE',
$t$Table 2— Minimum frontage boundary setbacks – structures [Primary | Secondary−Road | Secondary−Laneway, Pedestrian lane or Parkway | Secondary−Other] If 1.2m or less in height and involving building work that is: a swimming pool: 1.5m | 1.5m | 1.5m | 1.5m. If 2m or less ... a utility meter; or a utility structure, if screened from public view: BTB permitted (all). If 2.4m or less ... an external fixture: 1m | 1m | 1m | 1m. If over 2.4m ... an external fixture: 4m | 3m | 1.0m | 2m. If 3.0m or less ... an entry structure: BTB permitted (all). If 4.5m or less ... a weather control element for a carport: BTB permitted | BTB permitted | 0.5m | 1m. If 4.5m or less ... a covered outdoor room; or a balcony; or stairs, ramps or landings: 3m | 2.5m | 0.5m | 1.5m. If 8.5m or less ... a weather control element; or a privacy screen: 3m | 0.5m less than the setback for an external wall under Table A1 | 0.5m | 1.5m. Supplementary Provisions In Table 2: 1. Where structures exceed the height requirements stated in Table 2, or are not specifically listed in Table 2, the setback in Table 1 for Class 1 buildings applies. 2. If a structure is attached to a building, and the setback for the building specified in Table 1 is less than the setback for the structure specified in Table 2, the setback for the building in Table 1 applies to the structure. 3. Where a solid fence extends at least 1m above the level of the swimming pool deck (between the water and the frontage), the minimum setback for a swimming pool is BTB.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 45-46', 46, 'Chapter 2');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch2 A3.1 Table 3', 'TABLE',
$t$Table 3 — Minimum common boundary setbacks – external walls [Side boundary | Rear boundary] Class 1 buildings and Class 2 buildings: The ground floor, or that part of an external wall that is 4.5m or less in height: 1.5m | 4m. The second storey, or that part of an external wall that is over 4.5m and 8.5m or less in height: 1.5m | 3m. The third storey, or that part of an external wall that is over 8.5m in height: 2.5m | 3m. Secondary dwelling (single storey detached): That part of a detached secondary dwelling that is 4.5m or less in height: 1.5m | 2m. Class 10a buildings: If 3.5m or less in height and involving building work that is: a gatehouse that has a roof area of no more than 4m2: BTB permitted | BTB permitted. If 4.5m or less in height and involving building work that is: a garage, carport, laundry, or shed up to 9m total combined length along each common boundary: BTB permitted | BTB permitted. Supplementary Provisions In Table 3: ... 3. Where Class 10a buildings exceed the height requirements stated in Table 3, or are not specifically listed in Table 3, the setbacks for Class 1 buildings in Table 3 apply. 4. Setbacks under Table 3 may be determined using either: a. the number of storeys; or b. the height of the external wall at the relevant point. 5. For Table 3, a garage, carport, laundry, or shed may extend along two intersecting common boundaries, provided the total length along each common boundary does not exceed 9m.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 47-48', 48, 'Chapter 2');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch2 A3.1 Table 4', 'TABLE',
$t$Table 4 — Minimum common boundary setbacks – structures [Side boundary | Rear boundary] If 2m or less ... a utility meter; or a utility structure, if screened from public view: BTB permitted | BTB permitted. If 2.4m or less ... an external fixture: 0.5m | 0.5m. If 3.0m or less ... an entry structure: BTB permitted | BTB permitted. If 4.5m or less ... a weather control element for a carport: BTB permitted | BTB permitted. If 4.5m or less ... a covered outdoor room; or a balcony; or stairs, ramps or landings: 1m | 2m. If 4.5m or less ... a swimming pool: 1.5m | 1.5m. If 8.5m or less ... a weather control element; or a privacy screen: 0.5m | 2m. Supplementary Provisions In Table 4: 1. Where a weather control element is attached to a part of a building that is BTB, the minimum setback is BTB. 2. Where structures exceed the height requirements stated in Table 4, or are not specifically listed in Table 4, the setbacks in Table 3 for Class 1 buildings and Class 2 buildings apply. 3. If a structure is attached to a building, and the setback for the building specified in Table 3 is less than the setback for the structure specified in Table 4, the setback for the building in Table 3 applies to the structure. 4. Where a solid fence extends at least 1m above the level of the swimming pool deck (between the water and the adjoining premises), the minimum setback for a swimming pool is BTB.$t$,
 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 49-50', 50, 'Chapter 2');
SELECT define_provision('QHC@2026-09-01', 'Ch2 A4.1', 'ACCEPTABLE_SOLUTION',
$t$1. The maximum site cover for all buildings and structures does not exceed 50%. 2. When calculating site cover for a battle axe lot, the area of an access handle that forms part of the lot must be excluded from the calculation.$t$, 'VERIFIED_VERBATIM', 'Page 51', 51, 'Chapter 2');
